# Caddyfile: HTTPS + auth in front of the Tikal Toy Server
#
# tikal performs NO authentication of its own: anyone who can reach its port has full control of every connected toy.
# So it stays bound to localhost, and THIS reverse proxy is what protects it. It terminates TLS and enforces a
# username/password before any traffic reaches tikal.
#
# Caddy forwards the WebSocket upgrade automatically and does NOT inject an `Origin` header, which is what tikal needs
# (it rejects any handshake that carries one). Don't add one yourself.
#
# See ./README.md for the full walkthrough.
#
# Quick start:
#   1. Pick ONE of the two site blocks below, delete or comment out the other.
#   2. Fill in your domain (or LAN IP) and your password hash (step 3).
#   3. Generate the hash:   caddy hash-password (paste it after "tikal" — that word is the username).
#   4. Start tikal on its default localhost bind:   tikal-server
#   5. Start the proxy from this directory:         caddy run
#
# Clients then connect to  wss://<your-address>/  with HTTP Basic auth (username "tikal" + your password).
# The read-only status page is at https://<your-address>/ in a browser.


# Variant A: Public domain (Let's Encrypt certificate)
# Use this if you own a domain, can point it at this machine, and ports 80 + 443 are reachable from the internet.
# Caddy fetches and renews a real TLS cert for you, so clients get no certificate warnings.

toys.example.com {
    basic_auth {
        # username    bcrypt hash (generate with:  caddy hash-password)
        tikal $2a$14$Fq0m...replace-with-your-own-bcrypt-hash...
    }

    reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8142
}


# Variant B: LAN only (Caddy's internal CA, no domain needed)
# Use this if clients live on the same network, and you don't want to expose anything to the internet.
# Replace 192.168.1.50 with THIS machine's LAN IP. A hostname works too, if your clients can resolve it.
#
# `tls internal` makes Caddy issue the certificate from its own local CA. Each client device must trust that CA once.
#   - On THIS machine:  run `caddy trust` (adds the local CA to the OS store).
#   - On other devices: install Caddy's local-CA root certificate as a trusted root. Find it in Caddy's data directory
#   (run `caddy environ` and look for the data dir); on Windows it is typically: %AppData%\Caddy\pki\authorities\local\root.crt
#
# Delete Variant A above if you use this one.
#
# 192.168.1.50 {
#     tls internal
#     basic_auth {
#         tikal $2a$14$Fq0m...replace-with-your-own-bcrypt-hash...
#     }
#     reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8142
# }
